    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1c7a1bbc-47c8-5e16-803e-6063c96b4e9a",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{3t},\\,y=2{t}^{2}-4t,\\,0\\le t\\le 7.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t-4\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4t-4=0\\,⟹\\,t=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(1)=3{e}^{3}=3 e^{3}\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=1.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8ac9609f-7cf7-599a-b00b-5a8ddc26cc3f",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
