    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6b302301-7973-58c4-aa68-fa8c9c5fe7be",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2}-7t,\\,y=4{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal. Give an exact answer.\n\nThe normal to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4t-7,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-7 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =7 \\\\ t & =\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{7}{4}) & =12{(\\frac{7}{4})}^{2}=\\frac{147}{4}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=\\frac{7}{4}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
